{%- comment -%}
  PG Cart Imglink: the cart drawer's tap/scroll/layout fixes. Named for the
  first; the rest live here because overlay-group is at Shopify's hard limit of
  25 sections and this is the drawer's interaction file.

  1. TAPPING A LINE ITEM'S PICTURE OPENS THAT PRODUCT.
     The theme renders the thumbnail as <figure><picture><img> with no anchor
     above it -- the only link in the row is the small title. A DELEGATED
     listener is used rather than an <a> wrapper: the drawer is re-rendered
     wholesale on every cart change and pg-cart-smooth's morph re-uses surviving
     nodes, so a wrapper would have to be re-applied after every pass, and
     re-parenting under the morph is what produced the old upsell "spazzing".
     The href comes from the row's own product link, so the picture can never
     point somewhere the row does not. The R36S case is excluded by handle (both
     variants): it is a free gift with the console, not something to shop for.
     Bails on any existing control, on a text selection, and on anything but an
     unmodified primary click. Mobile only (760px).

  2. THE FREE-3rd-ORB CHOOSER OPENED WHERE NOBODY COULD SEE IT.
     The drawer DOES open. Measured at 390x844 with two orbs: .pg-unlock-pick
     becomes display:block, 137px tall, select inside. It is COVERED.
     .sticky-in-panel is position:sticky, bottom:-34px, z-index:5, opaque white,
     291px tall; the offer row spans y=535..799 and the pane starts at y=553.

     THE FIRST FIX WAS A SILENT NO-OP: it scrolled by the overlap, but this
     scroller IGNORES scrollTop (assigning it reads back 0 -- measured twice).
     The check that passed it only passed because that run happened to be clear
     already. A verification that cannot fail is not a verification.

     So the row is RAISED, not scrolled: while .is-open it takes z-index 6, one
     above the pane's 5. That depends on nothing but stacking order. It does put
     the picker over CHECKOUT, and that is the right trade -- the alternative is
     a shopper who cannot pick their free orb at all. It lasts only while the row
     is open, and the select opens as a native OS sheet, not an in-page dropdown.

  3. THE DEAD SCROLL, AND THE WHITE SPACE UNDER CHECKOUT.
     FREEZE: the scroller reports scrollHeight 1392 vs clientHeight 844 and
     ignores scrollTop, so with overscroll-behavior at its default `auto` a drag
     past the dead end CHAINS to the page behind and iOS spends a beat
     rubber-banding the wrong element. `contain` keeps the gesture inside.

     WHITE SPACE -- two wrong guesses first, recorded so they are not retried:
       - bottom:-34px is NOT the cause. Forcing it to 0 measured WORSE: the
         pane's bottom moved from y=844 (flush) to y=810, opening a real 34px
         gap. That offset cancels the drawer's own 34px bottom padding.
       - Chrome is not broken at all. At viewport heights 620/660/900/1000 the
         gap measured 0 every time. It was never findable on desktop.

     WHAT IT IS: .m6pn is position:fixed with height:100%, which on iOS Safari
     resolves against the LAYOUT viewport (full screen, including the strip
     behind the toolbar) while the shopper sees the shorter VISUAL viewport. So
     the drawer's bottom edge is off-screen behind the toolbar and the last
     visible thing is empty white drawer. Fixed by sizing .m6pn from
     window.visualViewport.height and keeping it in step, plus margin-top:auto on
     .sticky-in-panel so the pane pins to the bottom of the flex column when the
     content is short. In Chrome visualViewport.height == innerHeight, so the
     desktop that already measured correct does not move.

  4. THE FREE-SHIPPING BAR MATCHES THE WORDMARK BOX ABOVE IT.
     Measured, they agreed on nothing:
       .pg-cart-mark  x=32 w=329 radius 12px  padding 11px 10px 9px  #0B0710
       .nc-dship      x=55 w=284 radius 100px padding 7px 20px       transparent
     The bar now takes the same margins (so it tracks the wordmark box at any
     drawer width rather than being pinned to 329px), corners, padding and fill.

  5. STARS EITHER SIDE OF THE WORDMARK.
     Five four-point sparkles per side at five sizes, spread apart, at different
     heights and brightnesses, each with a purple halo and an offset twinkle.
     Injected rather than written into the markup because .nc-drawer-top is
     rendered by pg-drawer and rebuilt on every cart change; re-applied on each
     rebuild and aria-hidden, so the shop name is still announced once.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  /* --- 3. see note above --- */
  /* .m6pn is a SIBLING of #cart, not a descendant -- measured: a `#cart .m6pn`
     rule matched nothing. Listed bare, and #cart kept for the drawer itself. */
  /* GATED ON THE DRAWER BEING OPEN. These two declarations turn the drawer into
     a scroll container that swallows overscroll, which is right while it is on
     screen and wrong the rest of the time: #cart and .m6pn are position:fixed at
     the full size of the viewport on EVERY page, so unscoped this left a
     full-screen scrollable overlay sitting over the site permanently. Measured
     on a closed drawer: fixed, 0..844, overflow-y auto -- invisible and
     pointer-events:none, but still a scroller as far as the engine is concerned,
     which is the sort of thing iOS gets strange about at the end of a page.
     html.m6pn-open is the theme's own open-state class (confirmed: absent when
     closed, present when open). Reported as the page sticking at the bottom on
     mobile; not reproducible in headless Chromium, so this is the most likely
     cause removed rather than a confirmed fix. */
  html.m6pn-open #cart,
  html.m6pn-open .m6pn,
  html.m6pn-open #cart .l4ca{
    overscroll-behavior:contain;
    -webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;
  }
  /* --- 3. the pane sits at the bottom of the drawer column -- see note above --- */
  #cart .sticky-in-panel{margin-top:auto}

  /* --- 2. an OPEN chooser paints above the pinned pane -- see note above.
     Only while .is-open, so nothing overlaps CHECKOUT in the normal state. --- */
  #cart .pg-unlock.is-open{position:relative;z-index:6}
  #cart .pg-unlock.is-open .pg-unlock-pick{position:relative;z-index:6}

  /* --- 4. the free-shipping bar wears the wordmark box's shape --- */
  #cart .nc-dship,
  .nc-drawer-top .nc-dship{
    display:flex !important;
    align-items:center;
    justify-content:center;
    gap:8px;
    width:auto !important;
    margin:0 0 8px !important;
    padding:11px 10px 9px !important;
    border-radius:12px !important;
    background:#0B0710 !important;
    /* was #E4D9F0 -- a lavender at 11.5px/800 on #0B0710, which is the darkest
       surface in the drawer. It is the first thing in the cart and it was the
       hardest to read; white, and the truck with it. */
    color:#FFFFFF !important;
  }
  #cart .nc-dship svg,
  .nc-drawer-top .nc-dship svg{stroke:#FFFFFF !important;stroke-width:2.2 !important}

  /* --- 5. the stars ---
     Not a row of identical glyphs. The reference is a little scatter of
     four-point sparkles at five sizes, sitting at different heights and
     brightnesses, each with a purple halo -- so they read as glints rather than
     as punctuation. Sizes and offsets are per-star classes; the right-hand group
     is the same markup mirrored, so the pair frames the wordmark evenly. */
  #cart .pg-cart-star,
  .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star{
    display:inline-flex;
    align-items:center;
    gap:7px;
    vertical-align:middle;
    pointer-events:none;
    line-height:1;
  }
  #cart .pg-cart-star i,
  .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star i{
    font-style:normal;
    color:#C89BF0;
    line-height:1;
    /* the halo: a tight bright core and a wider soft bloom */
    text-shadow:0 0 6px rgba(200,155,240,.95), 0 0 16px rgba(150,80,220,.7);
  }
  #cart .pg-cart-star .pg-st-a,
  .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star .pg-st-a{font-size:21px;opacity:1;transform:translateY(-6px)}
  #cart .pg-cart-star .pg-st-b,
  .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star .pg-st-b{font-size:12px;opacity:.85;transform:translateY(6px)}
  #cart .pg-cart-star .pg-st-c,
  .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star .pg-st-c{font-size:16px;opacity:.95;transform:translateY(-2px)}
  #cart .pg-cart-star .pg-st-d,
  .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star .pg-st-d{font-size:9px;opacity:.7;transform:translateY(-8px)}
  #cart .pg-cart-star .pg-st-e,
  .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star .pg-st-e{font-size:14px;opacity:.8;transform:translateY(4px)}

  #cart .pg-cart-star.pg-cs-l,
  .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star.pg-cs-l{margin-right:11px}
  #cart .pg-cart-star.pg-cs-r,
  .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star.pg-cs-r{margin-left:11px;flex-direction:row-reverse}

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:no-preference){
    #cart .pg-cart-star .pg-st-a,
    .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star .pg-st-a{animation:pgStarTwinkle 3.4s ease-in-out infinite}
    #cart .pg-cart-star .pg-st-b,
    .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star .pg-st-b{animation:pgStarTwinkle 3.4s ease-in-out infinite .9s}
    #cart .pg-cart-star .pg-st-c,
    .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star .pg-st-c{animation:pgStarTwinkle 3.4s ease-in-out infinite 1.8s}
    #cart .pg-cart-star .pg-st-d,
    .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star .pg-st-d{animation:pgStarTwinkle 3.4s ease-in-out infinite 2.4s}
    #cart .pg-cart-star .pg-st-e,
    .nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-star .pg-st-e{animation:pgStarTwinkle 3.4s ease-in-out infinite 1.3s}
  }
  @keyframes pgStarTwinkle{
    0%,100%{filter:brightness(1)}
    50%    {filter:brightness(1.45)}
  }

  @media (max-width:760px){
    /* make the picture read as tappable: a pointer, a suppressed grey iOS tap
       flash (the row supplies its own feedback below), and no long-press image
       callout, which otherwise offers "Save Image" instead of following it */
    #cart .l4ca li figure{
      cursor:pointer;
      -webkit-tap-highlight-color:transparent;
      -webkit-touch-callout:none;
    }
    #cart .l4ca li figure img{pointer-events:none}
    /* a brief press state so the tap is acknowledged even before the page moves */
    #cart .l4ca li figure:active{opacity:.72;transition:opacity .06s ease}
  }
</style>
<script>
(function(){
  if (window.pgCartImgLink) return; window.pgCartImgLink = 1;

  /* Products whose picture is NOT a link. Matched on a handle FRAGMENT so the
     rule survives the handle being renamed around it (the case's handle carries
     a "-free-gift" suffix today). Both of the case's variants -- the free one
     and the paid spare -- share this handle, so both are covered. */
  var SKIP = ['r36s-protective-case'];

  function excluded(href){
    for (var i = 0; i < SKIP.length; i++){
      if (href.indexOf(SKIP[i]) > -1) return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  /* The row's own product link is the source of truth for where the picture
     should go: it is what the theme itself rendered for this line, so the
     picture can never point somewhere the row does not. */
  function hrefFor(li){
    var a = li.querySelector('a[href*="/products/"]');
    return a ? a.getAttribute('href') : '';
  }

  document.addEventListener('click', function(e){
    if (e.button !== 0 || e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey || e.shiftKey || e.altKey) return;
    if (!window.matchMedia('(max-width:760px)').matches) return;

    var fig = e.target.closest && e.target.closest('#cart .l4ca li figure');
    if (!fig) return;

    /* never take a tap that belongs to a control that was already there */
    if (e.target.closest('a, button, input, select, textarea, label, [role="button"]')) return;

    /* a tap that ends a text selection is not a tap on the picture */
    var sel = window.getSelection && window.getSelection();
    if (sel && sel.type === 'Range' && String(sel).length) return;

    var li = fig.closest('li');
    if (!li) return;

    var href = hrefFor(li);
    if (!href || excluded(href)) return;

    e.preventDefault();
    window.location.href = href;
  }, true);

  /* ---- 2. bring the free-orb chooser out from behind the pinned pane ---- */
  /* THE SCROLLER IS THE DRAWER, OR IT IS NOBODY.

     This used to fall back to `document.querySelector('#cart .m6pn') ||
     document.querySelector('.m6pn')` when the walk up from the item list found
     nothing scrollable. The first of those can never match -- .m6pn IS #cart,
     not a descendant of it -- so the fallback was always the second, which is
     the FIRST PANEL IN THE DOCUMENT: on most pages the nav drawer, not the
     cart. So whenever the cart drawer was not itself scrollable (which rule 2b
     in pg-cart-mobile deliberately makes the common case) this scrolled an
     unrelated, off-screen panel and reported success.

     Bounded to the cart now, and only while it actually has somewhere to go. */
  function scroller(){
    var cart = document.getElementById('cart');
    var el = document.querySelector('#cart .l4ca');
    while (el && el !== document.body){
      var s = getComputedStyle(el);
      if (/auto|scroll/.test(s.overflowY) && el.scrollHeight > el.clientHeight + 4) return el;
      if (el === cart) break;
      el = el.parentElement;
    }
    return null;
  }

  function reveal(){
    try {
      var pick = document.querySelector('#cart .pg-unlock.is-open .pg-unlock-pick');
      if (!pick) return;
      var pane = document.querySelector('#cart .sticky-in-panel');
      if (!pane) return;
      var pk = pick.getBoundingClientRect();
      var pn = pane.getBoundingClientRect();
      var over = pk.bottom - pn.top;          /* how far the picker runs under the pane */
      if (over <= 0) return;                  /* already clear: never jump for nothing */

      /* ONE SCROLL COMMAND, NOT TWO.

         This fired scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'}) and then immediately
         added to the same element's scrollTop. Two targets, issued in the same
         task, against a container the theme gives `scroll-behavior: smooth`
         (async-panels.css, .m6pn) -- so BOTH are animated, and the second
         retargets an animation the first had just started, from wherever it had
         got to rather than from where it began. reveal() is called twice per
         selection (260ms and 900ms), so the drawer is left chasing up to four
         overlapping animations, and a wheel or trackpad gesture arriving during
         any of them is overridden by the next frame of the one in flight: the
         cart slides on its own and refuses to be scrolled by hand.

         scrollIntoView is also the wrong one to keep. `block:'center'` targets
         a position the pinned pane can make unsatisfiable -- centre a picker
         taller than half the scrollport and its bottom is still under the pane
         -- and it scrolls EVERY scrollable ancestor, so on desktop it moves the
         page behind the drawer as well.

         What is actually wanted is one bounded nudge, by exactly the overlap
         that was measured, inside the drawer only. That is the scrollTop line;
         `behavior:'auto'` opts out of the theme's smooth default so the nudge
         lands in one frame and nothing is left animating for a gesture to fight.
         It is still best-effort: the z-index rule above is what makes the picker
         usable, this only tidies its position. */
      var sc = scroller();
      if (!sc) return;
      var want = Math.min(sc.scrollTop + over + 14, sc.scrollHeight - sc.clientHeight);
      if (want - sc.scrollTop < 1) return;
      if (sc.scrollTo) sc.scrollTo({top: want, behavior: 'auto'});
      else sc.scrollTop = want;
    } catch(e){ /* a failed scroll must never break the cart */ }
  }

  /* ---- 3. the drawer ends where the shopper's screen ends ----
     see the note above: height:100% on a fixed element is the LAYOUT viewport on
     iOS, which is taller than what is visible whenever the browser toolbar is
     up. visualViewport reports the visible box, so the drawer is sized from it
     and kept in step. Guarded on the API existing, and a no-op in browsers where
     the two are already equal. */
  function fitDrawer(){
    try {
      var vv = window.visualViewport;
      if (!vv) return;
      var aside = document.querySelector('.m6pn');
      if (!aside) return;
      /* only while the drawer is actually open, and only on a phone. This used
         to stamp an inline height on the closed drawer on every mobile page --
         a permanent inline size on a full-screen fixed overlay nobody was
         looking at. Same reasoning as the CSS gate above. */
      var open = document.documentElement.classList.contains('m6pn-open');
      if (!open || !window.matchMedia('(max-width:760px)').matches){
        aside.style.removeProperty('height');
        aside.removeAttribute('data-pg-h');
        return;
      }
      var h = Math.round(vv.height);
      if (aside.getAttribute('data-pg-h') === String(h)) return;   /* settle */
      aside.setAttribute('data-pg-h', String(h));
      aside.style.setProperty('height', h + 'px', 'important');
    } catch(e){}
  }
  fitDrawer();
  if (window.visualViewport){
    window.visualViewport.addEventListener('resize', fitDrawer);
    window.visualViewport.addEventListener('scroll', fitDrawer);
  }
  window.addEventListener('resize', fitDrawer);
  window.addEventListener('orientationchange', function(){ setTimeout(fitDrawer, 250); });
  /* the gate above means the drawer must be measured as it OPENS, not only when
     the viewport changes -- the open-state class lands on <html>, which the
     #cart observer below does not watch */
  document.addEventListener('click', function(e){
    if (!e.target.closest) return;
    if (!e.target.closest('[href="/cart"], .pgx-cart, .nc-cart-toggle, #pg-cartfab, [data-cart-toggle]')) return;
    [120, 400, 900].forEach(function(ms){ setTimeout(fitDrawer, ms); });
  }, true);

  /* ---- 5. stars either side of the wordmark ---- */
  function stars(){
    try {
      var mark = document.querySelector('.nc-drawer-top .pg-cart-mark');
      if (!mark) return;
      if (mark.querySelector('.pg-cart-star')) return;     /* already dressed */
      var word = mark.querySelector('.pg-wordmark');
      if (!word) return;
      var l = document.createElement('span');
      l.className = 'pg-cart-star pg-cs-l';
      l.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      /* five per side, no two the same size -- the CSS above spaces them out */
      l.innerHTML = '<i class="pg-st-d">✦</i>' +
                    '<i class="pg-st-b">✦</i>' +
                    '<i class="pg-st-a">✦</i>' +
                    '<i class="pg-st-e">✦</i>' +
                    '<i class="pg-st-c">✦</i>';
      var r = l.cloneNode(true);
      r.className = 'pg-cart-star pg-cs-r';
      mark.insertBefore(l, word);
      if (word.nextSibling) mark.insertBefore(r, word.nextSibling);
      else mark.appendChild(r);
    } catch(e){}
  }
  stars();

  /* the drawer is rebuilt wholesale on every cart change, which throws both of
     the above away -- re-apply on each rebuild */
  if (window.MutationObserver){
    var drawerEl = document.getElementById('cart') || document.querySelector('.m6pn');
    if (drawerEl){
      var q = false;
      new MutationObserver(function(){
        if (q) return;
        q = true;
        setTimeout(function(){ q = false; fitDrawer(); stars(); }, 120);
      }).observe(drawerEl, {childList: true, subtree: true});
    }
  }
  [400, 1200, 2600].forEach(function(t){ setTimeout(function(){ fitDrawer(); stars(); }, t); });

  document.addEventListener('click', function(e){
    if (!e.target.closest) return;
    if (!e.target.closest('.pg-unlock-add')) return;
    /* the panel is built after a fetch, so look once it can exist, then again
       in case the drawer re-rendered under it */
    setTimeout(reveal, 260);
    setTimeout(reveal, 900);
  }, true);
})();
</script>
{% schema %}
{"name": "PG Cart Imglink", "settings": [], "presets": [{"name": "PG Cart Imglink"}]}
{% endschema %}
